import tmp.sheaf.xif.paper.S2109M2cCarrierActual

/-!
# S2116: is the abstract commutator subgroup of `G_L = Gal(Qbar_p/L)` closed?

Layer: **O**.  Consumers: S2109's `M2cFirstMissing` / `carrierComparison_of_commutatorClosed`,
and through them S2027's `profiniteTorsionComparison`, `LocalArtinSourceData` and
`localReciprocityOnTorsion`.

## The question

For an open subgroup `U` of `G_v = Gal(Qbar_p/Q_p)`, i.e. `U = Gal(Qbar_p/L)` with
`L = FixedField U` finite over `Q_p`, S2109 isolates the exact first missing statement of
(M2c) as

  `M2cFirstMissing p := forall U, (commutator (localGaloisGroup p U)).topologicalClosure
                                = commutator (localGaloisGroup p U)`,

i.e. `[G_L, G_L]` is closed in `G_L`, equivalently the canonical map
`Abelianization U -> TopologicalAbelianization G_L` is a group isomorphism.

This file (1) proves the general compact-group mechanism that turns a *bounded
commutator width* into closedness, (2) constructs the whole (M2c) payload at the exact
S2027/S2109 types from a width hypothesis, (3) records the two deep group-theoretic
inputs precisely as `Prop`s -- never assumed -- and proves that they suffice, and (4)
proves that closedness of `[G_L, G_L]` is *equivalent* to bijectivity of the (M2c)
carrier map, so that the missing input is exactly the missing injectivity of that map.

## What is proved unconditionally

1. `commutatorWord`, `continuous_commutatorWord`, `commutatorWord_mem_commutator`:
   for *any* topological group `G`, the set of products of `N` commutators is the image
   of the compact space `(G x G)^N` under a continuous map.
2. `isClosed_commutator_of_boundedWidth` /
   `commutator_topologicalClosure_eq_of_boundedWidth`: in a compact Hausdorff
   topological group, if `[G,G]` is contained in the set of products of `N` commutators
   (`N` fixed), then `[G,G]` is closed.
3. `isClosed_commutator_of_isOpen`: in a topological group, if `[G,G]` is open then it is
   closed -- the second available general criterion.
4. `commutator_eq_closure_iUnion_commutatorWord`: `[G,G]` is the subgroup generated by
   the countable union of the compact sets `C_N` -- the exact place where closedness can
   fail (`C_N` is compact hence closed, but a countable union of closed sets need not
   be).
5. `commutatorClosed_iff_bijective`: for `G_L`, closedness of `[G_L,G_L]` is
   *equivalent* to bijectivity of `abelianizationToTopological`; no target is changed,
   neither side is replaced by the profinite carrier.
6. `abelianizationEquivGaloisGroupMaximalAbelian`: from closedness, the *abstract*
   abelianization `Abelianization U` is the Galois group `Gal(L^ab/L)` of the maximal
   abelian extension -- the carrier on which the tree's unconditional local reciprocity
   (S2098/S2102, via `PadicField.galAbEquiv`) identifies `O^× x Zhat`.
7. The end-to-end payload: `CommutatorWidthInput p` gives `M2cFirstMissing p`, gives the
   carrier comparison for every level, gives `profiniteTorsionComparison` and S2027's
   `LocalArtinSourceData` with the `carrier` field supplied, hence
   `localReciprocityOnTorsion`.

## The two deep inputs (recorded, never assumed)

* `TopologicallyFinitelyGenerated U` : `G_L` is topologically generated by finitely many
  elements.  For a local field this is the classical finiteness of the number of
  extensions of `L` of a given degree (Krasner's lemma, local field theory).
* `NikolovSegalCommutatorWidth` : in every topologically finitely generated profinite
  group, the abstract commutator subgroup is a product of boundedly many commutators, the
  bound depending on the group.  This is the commutator case of the Nikolov-Segal theory
  of verbal subgroups of finitely generated profinite groups
  (N. Nikolov, D. Segal, *On finitely generated profinite groups I: strong completeness
  and uniform bounds*, Ann. of Math. 165 (2007) 171--238; for the pro-`p` case see
  A. Jaikin-Zapirain, *On the verbal width of finitely generated pro-`p` groups*,
  Rev. Mat. Iberoam. 24 (2008) 617--630).  Both are absent from Mathlib v4.32.2 and from
  the tree.

`m2cFirstMissing_of_nikolovSegal` proves that these two inputs give `M2cFirstMissing`,
hence (M2c) at its exact type.

## Why the current tree and Mathlib cannot do better

* Mathlib has no theory of verbal subgroups or verbal width, no strong completeness and no
  Nikolov-Segal theorem.  `Mathlib.GroupTheory.Commutator.Basic` stops at the definition
  `commutator G = [(top, top)]` and the closure description `commutator_eq_closure`; the
  only topology around commutators in Mathlib is `TopologicalAbelianization`, which uses
  the closedness of the *closure* of `[G,G]`, not of `[G,G]` itself.
* Mathlib has no `n`-generation theory for profinite groups, no Krasner lemma and no
  finiteness statement for extensions of `p`-adic fields; hence even
  `TopologicallyFinitelyGenerated` is not available, although for `G_L` it is classical.
* Closedness is *not* a formal consequence of compactness (see the remark after
  `commutator_eq_closure_iUnion_commutatorWord`): compactness only gives that `[G,G]` is
  a countable union of compact sets and that it is dense in its closure; a countable
  union of closed sets need not be closed, and being "in the closure" is exactly the
  information visible in all finite quotients.  The specific group `G_L` must be used,
  and the known way to use it is the width / strong-completeness theory above.
* `G_L` is not `p`-adic analytic; the `p`-adic analytic theory applies only to its pro-`p`
  core, i.e. to the maximal pro-`p` quotient produced by the wild inertia, which is a
  Demushkin or free pro-`p` group.  That theory is
  not formalised in the tree (no Demushkin/Shafarevich presentation, no maximal pro-`p`
  quotient), and it would not suffice on its own: the wild inertia is closed of infinite
  index in `G_L`, so a transfer of commutator-closedness from it to `G_L` would need an
  argument of its own, and none is available.
* Strong completeness (Nikolov-Segal) is a *different* statement, about finite-index
  subgroups, and does not by itself give closedness of `[G,G]`, which in `G_L` has
  infinite index.  What is needed is the verbal-subgroup (width) half of the theory.

## Status (honest)

* `M2cFirstMissing` is **not** proved here, and neither is `CommutatorClosed U` for any
  concrete `U`.  What is proved is the unconditional reduction
  (bounded width) => (closedness) => ((M2c) at its exact type), plus the two missing
  inputs stated precisely.
* No `sorry`, `admit`, `axiom`, `opaque` or fabricated inhabitant.  The two inputs are
  hypotheses of theorems, never fields of data, and are never inhabited.
* Axiom probes are at the end of the file.

## Not run

Full Project build, target comparator, independent review.  The literature statements
quoted above were not re-verified verbatim against the published papers in this session
(network access was blocked); they enter only as *hypotheses* (`Prop`s), so no claim of
this file depends on their exact wording.

## References

* [NS] N. Nikolov, D. Segal, Ann. of Math. 165 (2007), 171--238.
* [NS2] N. Nikolov, D. Segal, C. R. Acad. Sci. Paris 337 (2003), 303--308.
* [JZ] A. Jaikin-Zapirain, Rev. Mat. Iberoam. 24 (2008), 617--630.
-/

